Day 248/250: Minimum Cost Path with Teleportations

Problem Statement #3651

Problem Statement Explantion:

You are given an m×n grid and an integer k, representing the maximum number of teleportations allowed.

Start: (0,0)
End: (m−1,n−1)

Movement Rules:

Normal Move: Move Right or Down to an adjacent cell. The cost of this move is the value of the destination cell: grid[i][j].
Teleportation: Move from your current cell (i,j) to any other cell (x,y) in the grid, provided that grid[x][y] <= grid[i][j]. The cost of a teleportation is 0.
Goal: Find the minimum cost to reach the bottom-right corner using at most k teleports.

Solution Approach:

The problem can be modeled as finding the shortest path in a weighted graph, but because we have a limited resource (teleports), we use Layered Dynamic Programming.

1. State Definition
We define dp[t][i][j] as the minimum cost to reach cell (i,j) using exactly t teleportations.

2. Transitions
There are two ways to reach a state (t,i,j):

From a Normal Move:

dp[t][i][j]=min(dp[t][i−1][j],dp[t][i][j−1])+grid[i][j]
This is the standard grid DP transition within the same teleportation layer.

From a Teleportation: If t>0, you could have arrived at (i,j) via a teleport from some cell (x,y) in layer t−1, provided grid[i][j]≤grid[x][y].

dp[t][i][j]=min(dp[t−1][x][y]) for all (x,y) where grid[x][y]≥grid[i][j]
3. Optimization
To avoid a O(m2⋅n2) search for every teleport, we use a Suffix Minimum array based on the grid values.
We track the minimum cost to reach any cell with a specific value V in the current layer.
We then pre-calculate the best cost among all cells with value ≥V. This allows each teleport transition to be calculated in O(1) after a single O(ValueRange) pass.

Here's the code:

import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int minCost(int[][] grid, int k) {
        int m = grid.length;
        int n = grid[0].length;
        int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE / 2;

        int maxVal = 0;
        for (int[] row : grid) {
            for (int val : row) {
                maxVal = Math.max(maxVal, val);
            }
        }

        int[][][] dp = new int[k + 1][m][n];
        for (int[][] layer : dp) {
            for (int[] row : layer) {
                Arrays.fill(row, INF);
            }
        }

        dp[0][0][0] = 0;

        for (int t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
            for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                    if (dp[t][i][j] == INF) continue;
                    
                    if (i + 1 < m) {
                        dp[t][i + 1][j] = Math.min(dp[t][i + 1][j], dp[t][i][j] + grid[i + 1][j]);
                    }
                    if (j + 1 < n) {
                        dp[t][i][j + 1] = Math.min(dp[t][i][j + 1], dp[t][i][j] + grid[i][j + 1]);
                    }
                }
            }

            if (t < k) {
                int[] minCostForValue = new int[maxVal + 1]; 
                Arrays.fill(minCostForValue, INF);

                for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
                    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                        if (dp[t][i][j] != INF) {
                            minCostForValue[grid[i][j]] = Math.min(minCostForValue[grid[i][j]], dp[t][i][j]);
                        }
                    }
                }

                int[] suffixMin = new int[maxVal + 2];
                suffixMin[maxVal + 1] = INF;
                for (int v = maxVal; v >= 0; v--) {
                    suffixMin[v] = Math.min(suffixMin[v + 1], minCostForValue[v]);
                }

                for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
                    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                        dp[t + 1][i][j] = Math.min(dp[t + 1][i][j], suffixMin[grid[i][j]]);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        int result = INF;
        for (int t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
            result = Math.min(result, dp[t][m - 1][n - 1]);
        }

        return result;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity:	O(k⋅(m⋅n+V))
Space Complexity: O(k⋅m⋅n)



